## Why You Can't Return Borrows to Locals

You cannot return a reference (`&T` or `&mut T`) to a value created inside the function — the value would be destroyed when the function ends, leaving a dangling reference.

### The Problem: Dangling Reference

```xiom
// INVALID: returns reference to local variable
// fn bad() -> &Vec[Int] {
//   let v = Vec[Int]();
//   v.push(1);
//   return &v;  // v destroyed here — reference would dangle!
// }
```

### Solution 1: Return the Value (Ownership)

```xiom
fn good() -> Vec[Int] {
  let v = Vec[Int]();
  v.push(1);
  return v;  // ownership moves to caller — safe!
}

let data = good();  // data now owns the Vec
```

### Solution 2: Take Reference as Parameter

```xiom
fn first_element(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Option[Int] {
  if v.len() == 0 { return None; }
  return Some(v[0]);  // returning a value, not a reference
}
```

### Solution 3: Return Reference to Parameter

```xiom
fn longest<'a>(a: &Vec[Int], b: &Vec[Int]) -> &Vec[Int] {
  if a.len() > b.len() { return a; }
  return b;
  // Safe — both references came from outside
}
```

### The Rule

A returned reference must come from one of the function's input references. You can reference parameters, but not locals.

### What to Do Instead

| You want to... | Do this |
|---------------|---------|
| Return new data | Return the owned value (`T`) |
| Return field of input | Take `&T`, return the field value |
| Return part of input | Take `&T`, return `&T` (lifetime tied to input) |
| Return computed value | Return the value directly |

